<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Gortyn</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.) · capital Gortyn</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Powerful city of the Mesara in southern Crete, famous for the Gortyn Code, the longest surviving early Greek law inscription.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gortyn grew in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> on the Lethaios river at the western edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesara-plain">Mesara</a> plain, near the older Minoan centre of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/phaistos">Phaistos</a>. By the 7th century BCE it was a Dorian city and, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/knossos">Knossos</a>, one of the two strongest powers on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gortyn-code">Gortyn Code</a>, inscribed c. 450 BCE on a wall later built into the Roman odeion, sets out laws on marriage, divorce, inheritance, adoption and the status of slaves and serfs. Gortyn was ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosmoi">kosmoi</a> magistrates and had men's messes. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it fought <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/knossos">Knossos</a> and destroyed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/phaistos">Phaistos</a> c. 150 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Roman conquest of 69 to 67 BCE, Gortyn, which had cooperated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, became the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/roman-cyrenaica">Crete and Cyrenaica</a>. It flourished under the Empire and the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gortyn.jpg" alt="Gortyn" width="320" height="233"><figcaption>Image: Marc Ryckaert (MJJR) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gortys_R02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gortyn</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cretan Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gortys, Gortyna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-384&amp;focus=gortyn" aria-label="Gortyn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5314 910 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gortyn lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5634 1166L5632 1166M5634 1166L5304 942M5634 1166L5629 1166"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5632 1166h0M5304 942h0M5629 1166h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5634 1166h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5634 1166h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-384&amp;focus=gortyn">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

